1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
 All right, welcome to week four.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,880
 Having conquered translating and rotating mechanical systems,

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:09,200
 this week we're gonna take a look at electrical circuits.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,120
 Now, I can already hear the screams of those of you

5
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:14,000
 who struggled in the 11, 13, 10.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,080
 Don't worry, I was one of you, in my case,

7
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:19,200
 it was the 11,300, but look, don't stress.

8
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,200
 Our approach in this course is going to be sufficiently different

9
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:24,520
 that you might actually come to enjoy modeling

10
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:26,720
 electrical circuits by the end of it.

11
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:28,920
 It turns out when you understand a few basic rules

12
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:31,760
 about how these things work, that modeling these systems

13
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:33,240
 is really, really easy.

14
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:35,920
 And in some ways, it's easier to see the flow of energy

15
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:38,760
 in an electrical circuit than it is in even a mechanical system.

16
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:41,320
 So, those of you who are mechanical engineers,

17
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:43,920
 you might actually come to enjoy modeling electrical circuits

18
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,520
 over the course of this week.

19
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:48,200
 So, let's start by talking about how we choose

20
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,680
 our states in electrical systems.

21
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:52,680
 Just like last week, where I didn't introduce you

22
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,640
 to just some random variables and tell you

23
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:56,320
 that's what we choose as states.

24
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,760
 I'm gonna now introduce you to the energy variables

25
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:00,080
 for this domain.

26
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,640
 And just like last week, those energy variables

27
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,840
 are gonna be what we choose as states.

28
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:08,000
 So, kind of as an analog to what we've already seen,

29
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,600
 the energy variables for electrical systems are charged

30
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,640
 and linked flux.

31
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,120
 Charge is a property you've probably already met before

32
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:17,400
 in your physics courses.

33
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:19,560
 It's like a fundamental property of the matter

34
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:21,040
 that makes up our world.

35
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:22,480
 And it kind of gives you a description

36
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:25,280
 of the presence or absence of electrons,

37
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:27,280
 things which have an excess of electrons

38
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:28,920
 that are called negatively charged,

39
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:30,720
 things which have a deficiency of electrons

40
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,320
 are called positively charged.

41
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:35,360
 And the opposite is true for protons.

42
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:38,160
 Things which have like charges have a tendency to repel

43
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:40,200
 and things with unlike charges,

44
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,120
 opposite charges have a tendency to attract.

45
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,120
 Flux though is a slightly more complicated topic.

46
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:49,920
 It's a quantity that kind of describes

47
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:53,200
 the strength or presence of a magnetic field.

48
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:54,680
 We'll talk a little bit more about this one.

49
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:56,160
 We go on to meet inductors,

50
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:00,120
 the component, the element for which this is most relevant.

51
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,920
 Either way, these are the things that we choose as states.

52
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,480
 The linked flux of any inductors in our systems

53
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:09,000
 and the charge stored in any capacitors in our systems.

54
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,360
 There's a nice kind of rule of thumb

55
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:12,600
 for how you choose states in electrical systems,

56
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:14,160
 how many you need.

57
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:16,080
 It's a little bit different to the one we met last week

58
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:17,880
 in the week before from mechanical systems

59
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:20,760
 where we had twice the number of degrees of freedom.

60
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:22,600
 That concept doesn't make a whole lot of sense

61
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:23,680
 for electrical circuits.

62
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,200
 So instead what we say is,

63
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,560
 we need one state for every energy storing element in the system.

64
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:30,880
 And by energy storing element,

65
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:32,720
 I don't mean things like sources.

66
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:35,240
 I don't mean resistors that dissipate energy.

67
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:37,560
 I mean inductors and capacitors.

68
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:39,000
 And to be more specific,

69
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,120
 I mean disconnected inductors and capacitors.

70
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:43,840
 Just as how in mechanical systems,

71
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:47,560
 we could sometimes have masses that were said to be connected

72
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:49,240
 and would count as one degree of freedom

73
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:51,560
 when we could kind of describe the position of one

74
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:54,360
 as a static function of the position of the other.

75
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:56,160
 This can also happen in electrical circuits

76
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:57,800
 for our inductors and capacitors.

77
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:01,520
 And we can end up with connected energy storing elements.

78
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:04,320
 So in general, if you see two or three or four

79
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,360
 in the capacitors in parallel

80
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:09,520
 or two or three or four inductors in series,

81
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:12,880
 those things should be treated as a single energy story element.

82
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:16,080
 And capacitors in parallel is one energy storing element.

83
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:19,800
 And any inductors in series is one energy storing element.

84
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:21,920
 When I say series in parallel here,

85
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:23,960
 I do actually mean the series in parallel

86
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:25,640
 that you met in the elect 13, 10,

87
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:28,520
 but more specifically, I mean the series in parallel

88
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:31,000
 that we met last week, the general definitions,

89
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,680
 the sharing of effort or sharing of flow.

90
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:36,040
 Speaking of effort and flow,

91
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:38,040
 we should probably talk about the power variables

92
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:40,120
 for this domain as well.

93
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:42,760
 Effort in the electrical domain is voltage,

94
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:45,240
 kind of the derivative of flux.

95
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:48,600
 And flow is current, kind of the derivative of charge,

96
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:50,920
 the motion of charged particles.

97
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:53,120
 All right, now that we understand the basics,

98
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:55,040
 I think we're okay to go on and see

99
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:56,480
 if we can count up some states

100
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:57,680
 and then move on to learning

101
00:03:57,680 --> 00:03:59,560
 about some fundamental components.
